# === Instrument runtime data: regenerated by the application, not versioned ===
# All of it is rewritten by the instrument itself, is specific to the crystal
# actually mounted, and produced hundreds of thousands of lines of diff churn per
# acquisition. A fresh clone gets it back by running Peak Detection (step 5 of the
# Quick Start) and one acquisition - both required before any measurement anyway.
#   Calibration_*MHz.txt  7.6 MB each, written by Peak Detection (Calibration.py
#                         via FileStorage.TXT_sweeps_save)
#   PeakFrequencies*.txt  written by Peak Detection; read at start-up by
#                         load_frequencies_file(), so run Peak Detection first
#   sweep_data/*.txt      one spectrum per overtone, rewritten every sweep - on
#                         this branch that covers the g<n>.txt raw-voltage files
#                         too; the Raw Data view has nothing to show until you
#                         acquire. A frozen reference copy of one of them lives in
#                         docs/impedance-analysis/reference-sweep/ for the worked
#                         example in ALGORITHM.md §11.
# The .py files in sweep_data/ are source and stay versioned - hence *.txt only.
software/openQCM/Calibration_5MHz.txt
software/openQCM/Calibration_10MHz.txt
software/openQCM/PeakFrequencies.txt
software/openQCM/PeakFrequenciesRT.txt
software/openQCM/sweep_data/*.txt

# === Raw sweep test datasets (peak-detection prototypes only) ===
research/peak-detection/**/*.txt

